    "summary": "Acoustic‑country vibe with soaring China Cat Sunflower",
    "blurb": "The set opens with a country‑rock acoustic groove before launching into psychedelic jams via China Cat Sunflower. Highlights include Green Green Grass Of Home, High Time, Alligator and an inspired The Other One sequence. The first half suffers from a muddy audience tape, while the second half is captured cleanly on soundboard.",
    "review": "June 21, 1969 at the Fillmore East finds the Dead deep in their early acoustic‑country phase, punctuated by flashes of the psychedelia that would soon dominate their sound. Jerry’s pedal‑steel work and soaring lead on China Cat Sunflower lift the set into a higher gear, leading seamlessly into an expansive Alligator jam that rolls into Drums, The Other One and Cryptical Envelopment before winding down with Cosmic Charlie. Bob supplies strong vocal leads in the acoustic moments, especially on Dire Wolf (though it isn’t on this particular setlist). The show’s musical core is solid, but the recording is a mixed bag: the first half is an audience tape that’s muddy and nearly unlistenable, whereas the latter portion comes from a crisp soundboard source, delivering the band’s energy in clear detail. For listeners who can tolerate the early‑set audio flaws, this document captures a pivotal moment in the Dead’s evolution.",
